Kinetosis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Nausea with movement, Pallor and diaphoresis, Visual-vestibular mismatch trigger

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-020
Reason for Visit: Intractable vomiting and severe dizziness following a boat trip.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an adult patient experiencing severe, unremitting nausea, active vomiting, and diaphoresis that has persisted for 4 hours following disembarkation from a prolonged boat trip. The patient is completely unable to tolerate oral fluids, posing a rapid and imminent risk of severe clinical dehydration and electrolyte imbalance. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to definitively rule out central neurological causes of acute vertigo (e.g., cerebellar stroke) and to administer immediate, potent parenteral or sublingual antiemetics to halt the vomiting cycle. This intervention is necessary to prevent an emergency hospital admission for intravenous rehydration, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: The room is completely spinning, and I can't stop retching every time I try to move my head.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 6 hours ago during a turbulent deep-sea fishing charter. The patient experienced a sudden onset of profound epigastric queasiness and frequent yawning, progressing rapidly to profuse cold sweating, hypersalivation, and severe nausea. The patient notes the symptoms were severely triggered by the passive locomotion of the boat and the visual-vestibular mismatch of being below deck. Despite being back on dry land for 4 hours, any movement of the head triggers intense dizziness and active vomiting, which provides only fleeting, temporary relief. Denies focal weakness, numbness, difficulty speaking, severe headache, or fever.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; completely unable to ambulate safely due to severe dizziness and nausea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High risk for rapid dehydration due to total inability to retain oral fluids.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, but lying rigidly still with eyes closed. Marked skin pallor and prominent diaphoresis visible on the forehead. Patient holds an emesis basin.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal neurological examination ruling out central vestibular pathology is paramount and assists in confirming the benign diagnosis.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a mild resting tachycardia consistent with early volume depletion and distress. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with normal active bowel sounds. Focused neurological examination is normal: cranial nerves are grossly intact, no spontaneous nystagmus when the head is stationary, and no dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing (performed supine). Otoscopic exam reveals clear external canals and intact, normal tympanic membranes. Epley maneuver not attempted due to active, severe nausea.
Environmental Observations: A bucket is next to the bed containing a moderate amount of clear, bilious vomitus. Room is warm and stuffy.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient attempted to take an over-the-counter ginger tablet but immediately vomited it up.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Kinetosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Cerebellar Stroke / TIA - Ruled out clinically by the absence of severe occipital headache, intact cranial nerve function, absence of dysmetria, lack of spontaneous resting nystagmus, and the overwhelmingly clear inciting trigger (passive locomotion on a boat).
Acute Labyrinthitis / Vestibular Neuritis - Less likely given the exact onset aligning precisely with a massive kinetic trigger rather than a viral prodrome, though it remains a consideration if symptoms persist beyond 24 hours of vestibular rest.
Gastroenteritis - Ruled out primarily due to the severe, prominent symptom of motion-triggered dizziness preceding the nausea, and the absolute absence of diarrhea or abdominal cramping.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with active fluid loss)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with severe, unremitting vomiting and vertigo following a massive kinetic trigger, resulting in an inability to tolerate oral fluids. Urgent on-site clinical evaluation was critical to perform a focused neurological exam to exclude central pathology, such as a posterior circulation stroke, which can mimic severe motion sickness. Upon confirming a benign but severe exacerbation of kinetosis, the immediate administration of an antiemetic was required to halt the hyperactive vagal response and prevent clinical dehydration. Providing this immediate stabilization safely in the home prevented an otherwise inevitable emergency department transport for IV fluids, rigorously fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually stat during the consultation, resulting in a rapid cessation of active retching. Prescribed Promethazine 25mg orally every 6 to 8 hours as needed for ongoing vestibular suppression and nausea.
Positive Behavioral Change: To successfully rehydrate without triggering the stretch receptors in your stomach that cause vomiting, take precisely one small sip of an electrolyte solution every 5 minutes using a teaspoon, rather than attempting to drink a whole glass at once. This slow, disciplined volume allows the fluid to be absorbed almost immediately without pooling in the stomach.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 48 hours if severe dizziness persists or if unable to maintain hydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you experience a sudden severe headache, difficulty speaking, weakness on one side of your body, double vision, or if you cannot pass urine for more than 12 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, based on the clear mechanism of injury (motion). Persistent neurological symptoms always warrant further investigation.
Clinical Course: The acute vomiting should cease immediately with the sublingual medication. The sensation of the "ground moving" (mal de debarquement) and generalized lethargy may linger for 12 to 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you vomit blood, or if your vomit looks like dark coffee grounds.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your brain received wildly conflicting signals between your eyes (seeing the stationary cabin) and your inner ear (feeling the turbulent boat motion), causing a massive reflex release of a chemical that triggers vomiting. The prescribed wafer dissolves under the tongue, bypassing your stomach entirely, to instantly block the vomit center in the brain.
Actionable Steps: Keep your room cool and well-ventilated, as heat and strong odors exacerbate nausea. Lie completely flat on your back and keep your head perfectly still, as any sudden rotation of the head will re-stimulate the inflamed balance centers in your ears.

SMS INFORMATION
Severe Motion Sickness (Kinetosis). Sublingual anti-nausea wafer given. Take promethazine for ongoing dizziness. Rest completely flat.
SMS EDUCATION
